{"id": "reasoning-892ccf00-6abc-5fc5-8fe9-cd804b88d217-0", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792348483714, "updatedAt": 1792348483714, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The probe is highly discriminating: at iteration 1, residual is tiny (4.6e-7 vs criterion 7.9e-5) and constraint is 0.0167 (needs to move). After the first Newton update, the constraint is satisfied exactly (0.0) but the residual JUMPS to 421031 — a huge residual. So the Newton step satisfies the control constraint but throws the equilibrium residual way off, and it never recovers. This is not a conditioning/stall issue — the very first update destroys equilibrium.\n\nThis suggests the augmented system solve gives a delta that satisfies the constraint but massively violates equilibrium — i.e., the linear solve is wrong. The probe uses MatrixClass.GENERAL already. Hmm, but the residual after update is huge (421031) while criterion is ~1e-4. That means the tangent used in the augmented matrix is inconsistent with the residual, OR the solve is garbage.\n\nWait — look at the sign convention. In the probe: residual = reduce_force(u, lam*F_prop - F_int). The augmented matrix is [K_red, -F_prop_red; row_jac, 0] with rhs [residual, constraint]. Newton: K_red dq - F_prop_red dlam = residual = lam*F_prop - F_int... wait, the linearization of R(q,lam) = lam*F_prop - F_int(q) is dR = -K_T dq + F_prop dlam. So the Newton system should be K_T dq - F_prop dlam = -R = F_int - lam*F_prop. The probe uses rhs = residual = lam*F_prop - F_int, which is -R. So the probe solves K dq - F dlam = lam*F - F_int, i.e., K dq - F dlam = R, which is the WRONG SIGN! The correct Newton: K dq - F dlam = F_int - lam*F_prop = -R.\n\nHmm wait, but the production solver presumably has the correct sign, and it also fails. Let me check the production code in nonlinear_static.py for the DC augmented system. Actually wait — maybe the production code has the same sign error! That would be a genuine production defect. Let me check.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me think about the sign of K_red. mpc_map.reduce_tangent(u, K_T, F_int - lam*F_prop, None) — the third arg is internal force? Let me look at the signature. reduce_tangent(u, K_T, F_int, ...) probably computes the reduced tangent including geometric terms from F_int. The probe passes (F_int - lam*F_prop) as the \"internal force\" for geometric tangent contributions. Hmm, that's the combined force for the geometric stiffness. OK.\n\nThe key observation: iteration 1 residual 4.6e-7 (essentially converged equilibrium at current lam), constraint 0.0167. One Newton step should move uz by -0.0167 with a small residual change. Instead residual explodes to 421031. That's a factor of ~1e9 explosion. The Newton step is clearly wrong — either sign error or the solve is broken.\n\nIf the sign of dq were wrong, the constraint would still be satisfied (row_jac @ dq = constraint regardless of overall sign of dq? No — the constraint row forces row_jac·dq = constraint, so uz moves correctly regardless. But the equilibrium rows: K dq - F dlam = rhs. If rhs has the wrong sign, then dq and dlam are... not simply negated because the constraint row pins the component along the control direction. The result: the equilibrium part is driven in the wrong direction, giving a huge residual.\n\nSo the probe itself may have a sign error. But the production solver fails the same way (maximum_iterations_reached at step 11). Let me read the production DC code in nonlinear_static.py to compare.", "summary": []}
{"id": "assistant-892ccf00-6abc-5fc5-8fe9-cd804b88d217", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792348483714, "updatedAt": 1792348483714, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The probe is highly discriminating: at iteration 1 the state is essentially in equilibrium (residual 4.6e-7 vs criterion 7.9e-5) and only the control constraint (0.0167) is unmet. One Newton update satisfies the constraint exactly but explodes the equilibrium residual to 4.2e5 — the linear solve drives equilibrium the wrong way. That points at the augmented system's sign/assembly, not conditioning. Let me read the production DC code."}], "source": "harness", "userDisplayContent": null}
