{"id": "effect-02fcc10e-1112-5a25-814a-4b7eb3bfa87b", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792348488899, "updatedAt": 1792348488930, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "offset": 1301, "limit": 180}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "content": "        from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n        self.T_linear = sparse.csr_matrix(T_linear, dtype=float)\r\n        self.entries = tuple(entries)\r\n        self.total_dofs = int(self.T_linear.shape[0])\r\n        self.n_red = int(self.T_linear.shape[1])\r\n\r\n    @property\r\n    def active(self) -> bool:\r\n        return bool(self.entries)\r\n\r\n    def apply_kinematics(self, u_linear: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Map a linear MPC displacement state ``u_lin`` to the nonlinear manifold.\"\"\"\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_linear, dtype=float).reshape(-1).copy()\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return u\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            delta_R = _nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation(theta_bar)\r\n            u[entry.slave_u_dofs] = u[entry.slave_u_dofs] + delta_R @ entry.eccentricity\r\n        return u\r\n\r\n    def to_linear_displacement(self, u_nonlinear: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Exact inverse diffeomorphism mapping ``u`` back to linear MPC coordinates.\"\"\"\r\n        u_lin = np.asarray(u_nonlinear, dtype=float).reshape(-1).copy()\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return u_lin\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u_lin)\r\n            delta_R = _nonlinear_minus_linear_rotation(theta_bar)\r\n            u_lin[entry.slave_u_dofs] = (\r\n                u_lin[entry.slave_u_dofs] - delta_R @ entry.eccentricity\r\n            )\r\n        return u_lin\r\n\r\n    def jacobian(self, u_current: np.ndarray) -> Any:\r\n        \"\"\"Return the state-dependent kinematic Jacobian ``J(u) = du / dq``.\"\"\"\r\n        from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return self.T_linear\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        row_blocks = []\r\n        col_blocks = []\r\n        data_blocks = []\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_bar, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            delta_A = A + entry.r_skew  # A(theta_bar, r_0) - (-skew(r_0))\r\n            corr = sparse.csr_matrix(delta_A) @ entry.theta_proj\r\n            coo = corr.tocoo()\r\n            if coo.nnz:\r\n                row_blocks.append(entry.slave_u_dofs[coo.row])\r\n                col_blocks.append(coo.col)\r\n                data_blocks.append(coo.data)\r\n        if not data_blocks:\r\n            return self.T_linear\r\n        delta_T = sparse.coo_matrix(\r\n            (\r\n                np.concatenate(data_blocks),\r\n                (np.concatenate(row_blocks), np.concatenate(col_blocks)),\r\n            ),\r\n            shape=(self.total_dofs, self.n_red),\r\n            dtype=float,\r\n        ).tocsr()\r\n        return (self.T_linear + delta_T).tocsr()\r\n\r\n    def reduce_force(self, u_current: np.ndarray, full_force: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Return ``J(u)^T full_force`` without materializing ``J(u)``.\"\"\"\r\n        f = np.asarray(full_force, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return np.asarray(self.T_linear.T @ f, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        f_mod = f.copy()\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            f_b = f[entry.slave_u_dofs]\r\n            if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n                continue\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_bar, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            delta_A = A + entry.r_skew\r\n            f_mod[entry.slave_theta_dofs] += delta_A.T @ f_b\r\n        return np.asarray(self.T_linear.T @ f_mod, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n\r\n    def geometric_tangent(\r\n        self,\r\n        u_current: np.ndarray,\r\n        full_imbalance: np.ndarray,\r\n    ) -> Any:\r\n        \"\"\"Return ``K_mpc,geom(u, F_int - F_ext) = sum_b Theta_b^T H_b Theta_b``.\"\"\"\r\n        from scipy import sparse\r\n\r\n        if not self.entries or self.n_red == 0:\r\n            return sparse.csr_matrix((self.n_red, self.n_red), dtype=float)\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        imbalance = np.asarray(full_imbalance, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        terms = []\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            if entry.theta_proj.nnz == 0:\r\n                continue\r\n            f_b = imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs]\r\n            if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30:\r\n                continue\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            H_b = rotating_mpc_geometric_hessian(\r\n                theta_bar, entry.eccentricity, f_b\r\n            )\r\n            terms.append(entry.theta_proj.T @ sparse.csr_matrix(H_b) @ entry.theta_proj)\r\n        if not terms:\r\n            return sparse.csr_matrix((self.n_red, self.n_red), dtype=float)\r\n        total = terms[0]\r\n        for term in terms[1:]:\r\n            total = total + term\r\n        return total.tocsr()\r\n\r\n    def reduce_tangent(\r\n        self,\r\n        u_current: np.ndarray,\r\n        K_T: Any,\r\n        full_imbalance: Optional[np.ndarray] = None,\r\n        K_ext: Optional[Any] = None,\r\n    ) -> Any:\r\n        \"\"\"Return ``J(u)^T (K_T - K_ext) J(u) + K_mpc,geom(u, full_imbalance)``.\"\"\"\r\n        J = self.jacobian(u_current)\r\n        K_red = (J.T @ K_T @ J).tocsr()\r\n        if K_ext is not None and getattr(K_ext, \"nnz\", 0):\r\n            K_red = (K_red - (J.T @ K_ext @ J)).tocsr()\r\n        if full_imbalance is not None:\r\n            K_geom = self.geometric_tangent(u_current, full_imbalance)\r\n            if K_geom.nnz:\r\n                K_red = (K_red + K_geom).tocsr()\r\n        return K_red\r\n\r\n    def transfer_slave_imbalance_to_masters(\r\n        self,\r\n        u_current: np.ndarray,\r\n        full_imbalance: np.ndarray,\r\n    ) -> np.ndarray:\r\n        \"\"\"Transfer slave forces/moments to master shell DOFs for support reactions.\"\"\"\r\n        imbalance = np.asarray(full_imbalance, dtype=float).reshape(-1).copy()\r\n        if not self.entries:\r\n            return imbalance\r\n        u = np.asarray(u_current, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\r\n        for entry in self.entries:\r\n            f_b = imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs].copy()\r\n            m_b = imbalance[entry.slave_theta_dofs].copy()\r\n            theta_bar = entry.master_rotation(u)\r\n            A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_bar, entry.eccentricity)\r\n            moment_covector = m_b + A.T @ f_b\r\n            for m_u, m_theta, w in entry.masters:\r\n                imbalance[m_u] += w * f_b\r\n                imbalance[m_theta] += w * moment_covector\r\n            imbalance[entry.slave_u_dofs] = 0.0\r\n            imbalance[entry.slave_theta_dofs] = 0.0\r\n        return imbalance\r\n\r\n\r\ndef has_nonlinear_rotating_mpc(model: \"FEModel\") -> bool:\r\n    \"\"\"Return True if ``model`` contains active rotating-arm beam-shell MPCs.\"\"\"\r\n    mesh = getattr(model, \"mesh\", None)\r\n    elements = getattr(mesh, \"elements\", None)\r\n    if not elements:\r\n        return False\r\n    for element in elements.values():\r\n        getter = getattr(element, \"get_nonlinear_mpc_descriptor\", None)\r\n        if callable(getter) and getter(mesh) is not None:\r\n            return True\r\n    return False\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _reject_downstream_rotating_slave_translation_dependencies(\r\n    model: \"FEModel\",\r\n    entries: Tuple[NonlinearRotatingMPCEntry, ...],\r\n) -> None:\r\n    \"\"\"Fail closed when an admitted affine equation consumes a rotating slave translation.\r\n\r\n    The rotating map corrects only each descriptor's own slave translations.\r\n    Any other admitted affine equation that uses one of those translations\r\n    as a nonzero master would silently retain the linearized lever-arm value\r\n    in the returned physical displacement, so such compositions are refused\r\n", "numLines": 180, "startLine": 1301, "requestedOffset": 1301, "requestedLimit": 180, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "180 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\src\\anysolver\\corotational.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-dfdf7e0a-7f2e-5ad9-8b16-aa16b610f698-0", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792348519537, "updatedAt": 1792348519537, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now the sign analysis:\n\n- Residual (production): `residual = reduce_force(u, F_external - F_int)` = J^T (F_ext - F_int) = R(q, lam).\n- dR/dq: R = J(u(q))^T (F_ext(u(q)) - F_int(u(q))). \n\ndR/dq = J^T (K_ext - K_T) J + (dJ^T/dq)(F_ext - F_int).\n\nreduce_tangent returns J^T (K_T - K_ext) J + K_geom(u, F_int - F_ext).\n\nNote: K_geom(u, full_imbalance) = sum Theta^T H_b Theta where H_b is the geometric Hessian wrt the imbalance passed. Production passes `F_int - F_external` as full_imbalance. So reduce_tangent = J^T(K_T - K_ext)J + K_geom(u, F_int - F_ext).\n\nHmm, what is the correct Newton tangent for R = J^T(F_ext - F_int)?\n\ndR/dq = J^T(K_ext - K_T)J + d/dq[J^T] (F_ext - F_int).\n\nThe geometric term: d/dq[J^T v] where v = F_ext - F_int. The map's geometric_tangent(u, imbalance) presumably computes d/dq[J^T · (-imbalance)]? Let's think: for internal force equilibrium with residual R_int = J^T(F_ext - F_int), the standard Newton tangent is -d(F_int_reduced)/dq = J^T K_T J + geometric. Actually the standard: g(q) = J^T F_int(u(q)) (reduced internal force). dg/dq = J^T K_T J + K_geom where K_geom = dJ^T/dq F_int (with appropriate sign). \n\nThe map's geometric_tangent docstring: \"K_mpc,geom(u, F_int - F_ext) = sum_b Theta_b^T H_b Theta_b\". So it's designed to be added to J^T K_T J to form the tangent of the reduced INTERNAL force g(q) = J^T F_int, i.e., dg/dq = J^T K_T J + K_geom(u, F_int - F_ext)? Hmm, the imbalance passed is F_int - F_ext... For the internal force tangent, the geometric contribution is d/dq[J^T F_int]. Writing J^T F_int = J^T(F_int - F_ext) + J^T F_ext; if F_ext is dead (constant in u), d/dq[J^T F_ext] ≠ 0 in general because J depends on q! Hmm, but for the rotating map, J depends on q only through master rotations theta. And J^T F_ext... the derivative involves the same H_b structure with force f_b = F_ext at slave dofs? \n\nActually let me not go down the full derivation. The key empirical fact from the probe: at iteration 1, R ≈ 0 (4.6e-7), constraint = 0.0167. The Newton solve should produce a small-ish dq that moves uz by -0.0167 while keeping R ≈ 0. Instead R explodes to 4.2e5. Since R was ~0 and the step is small in the control direction (0.0167 m), the true tangent would give a residual after the step of order (tangent error) × step. An explosion by 1e9× means the solved delta is wildly wrong — the linear system as assembled does not represent the local linearization.\n\nPossibilities:\n1. Sign error in rhs: solving K_red dq - F_prop_red dlam = R instead of = -R' (with correct tangent convention). If K_red is the tangent of the reduced internal force g (dg/dq), then Newton for R = J^T(F_ext) - g = 0: dg/dq dq = d(J^T F_ext)... with dead loads F_ext constant in u but J varies: R = J^T F_ext - g. dR/dq = dJ^T/dq F_ext - dg/dq. Hmm, the J^T F_ext term derivative is NOT zero. But wait — is it? For dead loads applied at shell nodes... J's q-dependence comes only through master rotations theta_bar(u). J^T F_ext: the slave rows of J get correction delta_A @ theta_proj. dJ^T/dq F_ext involves Hessian of the lever arm weighted by the force at slave dofs. The map's geometric_tangent(u, imbalance) computes exactly sum Theta^T H_b(imbalance at slave_u_dofs) Theta. So d/dq[J^T v] = K_geom(u, v) presumably (with H_b built from f_b = v[slave_u_dofs]).\n\nSo: R(q) = J^T (F_ext - F_int). \ndR/dq = K_geom(u, F_ext) - [J^T K_T J + K_geom(u, F_int)]... wait dg/dq where g = J^T F_int: dg/dq = J^T K_T J + dJ^T/dq F_int = J^T K_T J + K_geom(u, F_int).\nd(J^T F_ext)/dq = K_geom(u, F_ext) (for dead loads).\nSo dR/dq = K_geom(u, F_ext) - J^T K_T J - K_geom(u, F_int) = -[J^T K_T J + K_geom(u, F_int - F_ext)] = -reduce_tangent(u, K_T, F_int - F_ext) (with K_ext=None, dead loads).\n\nSo dR/dq = -K_red where K_red = reduce_tangent as called in production (dead load case). And dR/dlam = J^T F_prop = F_prop_red.\n\nNewton: R + dR/dq dq + dR/dlam dlam = 0 → -K_red dq + F_prop_red dlam = -R → K_red dq - F_prop_red dlam = R.\n\nThat's EXACTLY what production solves: [K_red, -F_prop_red] [dq;dlam] = [R; constraint]. So the sign is correct for dead loads. Good — so no sign error.\n\nThen why does the residual explode? The magnitude: R after update = 421031 while criterion ~1e-4·reference... wait criterion is 7.9e-5 = tolerance*reference where reference = norm of reduced F_ext ~ 7.9e3. So R=421031 is huge compared to forces ~8000. The update moved the state far. But the constraint is satisfied exactly (5.5e-17). Hmm — the constraint row forces row_jac·dq = 0.0167. So uz moved by exactly 0.0167. Yet the residual exploded. That means dq has huge components in other directions — the augmented matrix is nearly singular in those directions, or K_red is wrong.\n\nWait, actually, maybe the issue: K_red as tangent of internal force includes K_geom(u, F_int - F_ext). At the current state, F_int - F_ext ≈ 0 (equilibrium!). So the geometric term ≈ 0. Fine.\n\nHmm, but wait — actually there's a subtlety: the reduced residual R = J^T(F_ext - F_int) ≈ 4.6e-7 ≈ 0. The state is in equilibrium. The control row: row_jac = row_full @ J(u). The Newton system:\n\n[K_red, -F_prop_red; row_jac, 0] [dq; dlam] = [R≈0; 0.0167]\n\nIf R ≈ 0, the solution satisfies K_red dq = F_prop_red dlam (equilibrium rows) and row_jac dq = 0.0167. This is the standard DC step: find the equilibrium direction along the control displacement. This should give a reasonable dq IF K_red is a good tangent. The residual after the step being 4e5 means the linearization is very wrong, OR K_red is ill-conditioned/singular and the solve produces garbage that still satisfies the two linear conditions.\n\nNote the probe uses lstsq to recover q from u_initial — recovery_error 9.4e-17, fine.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, is K_red singular? At a limit point in load control... but this is displacement control; K_red singular would mean a snap-through in displacement control too (singular tangent including the control row). The lam trajectory: direct goes 1.45 → 2.80 over 10 steps, increasing. Step 11 target -0.1833. If the structure is near a snap-through (lam decreasing back), the augmented matrix could be near-singular. But the residual explosion by 1e9 in ONE step of 0.0167 suggests the solve returned enormous dq components that cancel in the row_jac projection.\n\nLet me think about \"GENERAL vs SYMMETRIC\": production uses `MatrixClass.GENERAL if general_tangent else MatrixClass.SYMMETRIC_INDEFINITE`. The probe used GENERAL explicitly. So the probe already discriminates: GENERAL does not fix it. The supervisor said \"do not assert it causes the observed stall unless the recorded probe discriminates. The manual probe already uses GENERAL. Verify actual backend if relevant.\"\n\nSo the probe shows: even with GENERAL, the Newton step from a well-equilibrated state explodes the residual. This is not a matrix-class issue. Something else is wrong. Options:\n\n1. K_red (the reduced tangent) is wrong at this state — e.g., the geometric term with imbalance F_int - F_ext ≈ 0 is fine, but J^T K_T J might be wrong... but it worked for 10 steps.\n\n2. The tangent is fine but the system is genuinely near-singular, so the solve gives a huge dq with tiny row_jac projection. The residual explosion would then be physical (jumping to a far-away state). In that case bounded subdivision is the adopted remedy: subdivide the control interval so the Newton step stays in the basin.\n\nWait, but actually let me reconsider: is the augmented system near-singular? At a displacement-control limit point (lam max), the augmented matrix [K, -F; row, 0] becomes singular. The direct case: lam increasing 2.76 → 2.80 → step 11 fails. Maybe lam is near its maximum (a snap-through/limit point in the DC sense). The probe's iteration trajectory shows lam bouncing 2.3–4.3 wildly — consistent with a near-singular augmented system: tiny changes push lam everywhere while the constraint stays satisfied.\n\nHmm, but the residual norm exploding to 4e5 with criterion 8e-5... the state jumps far away. With a near-singular K_red, the solve produces huge dq. Subdividing the control interval does NOT help if the tangent itself is singular at the equilibrium point — the linear solve is rank-deficient regardless of step size. Actually subdivision changes the constraint rhs (smaller increment), but if the matrix is near-singular, dq = A^{-1}[0; small] can still be huge because A^{-1} is huge in the near-null direction. Hmm, but not exactly: if A is near-singular with near-null vector n, then dq ≈ (n·rhs)/(n^T A n) n. With rhs = [R≈0; eps], the component along n scales with eps/(n^T A n). Subdivision reduces eps → reduces dq magnitude proportionally. So subdivision CAN keep the step in the local basin if the nonlinearity is the issue (the linearization is only locally valid). The residual after one step ~ O(dq²) curvature. If dq shrinks by 2×, the residual shrinks ~4×. But the observed residual 4e5 vs criterion 8e-5 needs a reduction of 5e9 — that needs dq reduced by ~7e4× — way beyond min-step subdivision. Unless the near-singularity is the problem and Newton with exact tangent would still converge quadratically once close... but it's not converging at all — residual bounces 4e5, 1.5e5, 4.5e4, ... 9e5. It's diverging/chaotic.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider. Maybe the tangent K_red is actually WRONG at this state — specifically the geometric term. Production passes full_imbalance = F_int - F_external to reduce_tangent. At equilibrium F_int - F_ext ≈ 0, so K_geom ≈ 0. But the CORRECT tangent of the reduced internal force g = J^T F_int is J^T K_T J + K_geom(u, F_int) — with F_int, NOT the imbalance! Wait no. Let me redo this.\n\nHmm wait, I need to be careful about what function's tangent we need. We need dR/dq where R = J^T(F_ext - F_int).\n\nR = J^T F_ext - J^T F_int.\n\nd/dq [J^T F_int]: J^T K_T J + (dJ^T/dq)·F_int. The second term: derivative of the map's force-reduction wrt q. The map's geometric_tangent(u, imbalance) computes sum_b Theta_b^T H_b(theta_bar, eccentricity, f_b) Theta_b where f_b = imbalance[slave_u_dofs]. What is H_b? \"rotating_mpc_geometric_hessian(theta_bar, eccentricity, f_b)\" — presumably the Hessian of the lever-arm correction such that d/dq[J^T v] = geometric_tangent(u, v)? Or = -geometric_tangent(u, v)?\n\nThe docstring of geometric_tangent: \"K_mpc,geom(u, F_int - F_ext) = sum_b Theta_b^T H_b Theta_b\". And reduce_tangent: \"J(u)^T (K_T - K_ext) J(u) + K_mpc,geom(u, full_imbalance)\". The intended meaning: reduce_tangent returns the tangent of the reduced INTERNAL-minus-external force, i.e., d/dq[J^T(F_int - F_ext)] = J^T(K_T - K_ext)J + K_geom(u, F_int - F_ext). So d/dq[J^T v] = K_geom(u, v) with the sign convention such that this holds. I.e., geometric_tangent(u, v) = d/dq[J^T v].\n\nThen: dR/dq = d/dq[J^T F_ext] - d/dq[J^T F_int] = K_geom(u, F_ext) - J^T K_T J - K_geom(u, F_int) = -[J^T K_T J + K_geom(u, F_int - F_ext)] = -reduce_tangent(u, K_T, F_int - F_ext) (dead loads, K_ext=None).\n\nConsistent with before: dR/dq = -K_red, and the production Newton system K_red dq - F_prop_red dlam = R is correct.\n\nBUT: there's a subtlety — the geometric term uses imbalance = F_int - F_ext ≈ 0 at equilibrium. The correct decomposition: -dR/dq = J^T K_T J + K_geom(u, F_int) - K_geom(u, F_ext). Production computes J^T K_T J + K_geom(u, F_int - F_ext) = J^T K_T J + K_geom(u, F_int) - K_geom(u, F_ext) — since K_geom is LINEAR in the force argument (H_b linear in f_b), these are identical! OK so no issue. Good.\n\nSo the assembly is mathematically correct. Then the failure is genuine: near-singular tangent / diverging Newton at step 11. Hmm, but wait — is it really? Let me rec