e equilibrium manifold, possibly within the convergence basin. Actually with R≈0 exactly and pure constraint rhs, dq = A^{-1}[0;eps] — scales linearly with eps. The resulting residual after the step: R(u_new) ≈ dR/dq·dq + O(dq²) = -K_red·dq + ... = F_prop_red·dlam - ... hmm, by construction K_red dq - F_prop_red dlam = 0, so the LINEARIZED residual change is zero (since rhs R≈0). The actual residual 4e5 is pure second-order + tangent inconsistency: R(u_new) = R + dR·dq + ½dq^T H dq... = ½ dq^T H dq (second order!). So residual ~ O(dq²). Subdivision by factor 2 → residual drops 4×. To go from 4e5 to below 8e-5 (criterion) needs dq reduced by ~7000× — min-step would stop far earlier. UNLESS the quadratic coefficient is misleading and Newton converges once the step is small enough to be in the quadratic basin: the basin requires ||dq|| small enough that O(dq²) < O(dq), i.e., the local convergence radius. If at dq_0 (full step) the second-order residual is 4e5 and the first-order (tangent) residual prediction is ~0... the convergence radius depends on the Hessian-to-tangent ratio. ||dq|| for the full step: uz component 0.0167 but total ||dq|| could be huge (the garbage direction). If ||dq|| ~ 1 (huge), then reducing to ~1e-3 might enter the basin. Subdivision by 2^k with min-step... the \"existing min-step control\" — what is the min step? Need to check the DC settings.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider whether the tangent is actually consistent. The R4 test verified the complete reduced derivative vs central FD — at some state. If the tangent is consistent, Newton from R≈0 with a small constraint should give a step whose residual is second-order. The observed 4e5 means ||dq||² × H is huge — either ||dq|| huge (ill-conditioning) or H huge (tangent wrong).\n\nLet me quickly estimate ||dq|| from the probe: we know dlam=1.56. If the near-null direction has F_prop_red·dlam balanced by K_red·dq... I can't get ||dq|| without running.\n\nGiven the constraints (no broad invest