{"id": "reasoning-12db5341-bc4c-593a-9d0f-805e7df56688-0", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792348547358, "updatedAt": 1792348547358, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now I have the full picture of the DC loop. Design for the bounded subdivision (rotating-MPC, contact-free route only):\n\nScope condition: `rotating_mpc_map is not None and contact_manager is None` (contact-free means no active contact manager at all — no augmented, no moving tool).\n\nImplementation approach: wrap the per-step Newton attempt in a subdivision retry. Restructure minimally:\n\nCurrently:\n```\ntarget = initial_control + target_increment * local_step_index / num_steps\n```\nThe step goes from current control value (== previous milestone, since steps are accepted sequentially) to `target`.\n\nPlan: introduce an inner loop over sub-interval attempts:\n\n```\nsubdivision_enabled = rotating_mpc_map is not None and contact_manager is None\n...\nprev_target = previous milestone (initial_control + target_increment*(local_step_index-1)/num_steps)\n```\n\nBut careful: on a resumed run, initial_control is the restart state's control value and target_increment = target_total - initial_control; milestones are initial_control + target_increment*k/num_steps for k=1..num_steps. The previous milestone for local_step_index=1 is initial_control (the current state). Good.\n\nMinimal-diff approach: convert the body of the step loop into a retry over `subdivision` counts. That's a large refactor of a big block. Alternative: keep the existing loop structure, but on failure (maximum_iterations_reached only, subdivision enabled), instead of breaking, restore state and re-enter a nested attempt loop for the same milestone with subdivided targets.\n\nCleaner: wrap the existing per-step attempt in a `while True` loop with a `sub_interval` list. Let me restructure:\n\n```\n# before the step loop:\nsubdivision_adaptive = rotating_mpc_map is not None and contact_manager is None\nmax_control_cutbacks = 8  # bounded: sub-increment >= scheduled/256\ncontrol_cutbacks_total = 0\ncontrol_attempts_total = 0\n\nfor local_step_index in ...:\n    ...\n    milestone = initial_control + target_increment * local_step_index / num_steps\n    segment_start = current control value at step start (== previous milestone)\n    subdivisions = 1\n    cutbacks_this_step = 0\n    while True:\n        sub_target = segment_start + (milestone - segment_start) / subdivisions  # next sub-target\n        ... run existing Newton attempt toward `target = sub_target` ...\n        if failure_reason is None:\n            if abs(current - milestone) <= tolerance*target_scale (or sub_target == milestone): \n                # milestone reached -> commit step record and break to next step\n                break (out of while)\n            else:\n                # accepted a proper sub-interval; continue the while for the next sub-target\n                segment_start = current\n                continue\n        if failure_reason == \"maximum_iterations_reached\" and subdivision_adaptive and cutbacks_this_step < max:\n            # discard trial, restore exactly, subdivide\n            restore q/lam/states\n            subdivisions *= 2\n            cutbacks_this_step += 1\n            failure_reason = None\n            continue\n        break # genuine failure\n```\n\nBut the existing code commits the step record (steps.append, history, snapshots, progress callback) inside the step loop right after the attempt. With subdivisions, intermediate accepted sub-intervals also need to be recorded? The supervisor: \"Preserve every original scheduled control milestone, so half6+resume6 and full12 trace the same intended targets with any accepted subdivisions inserted. Do not claim exactly12 accepted steps. Record attempts/cutbacks/stop reason.\"\n\nSo accepted subdivisions are INSERTED as additional step records. The test asserts:\n- `len(result.snapshots) == len(result.steps)` — fine.\n- control_values strictly decreasing — fine.\n- resume test: `len(resumed_steps) == len(full_tail) == DC_STEPS // 2` — PROBLEM! If subdivisions insert extra accepted steps, the resumed run's step count may exceed 6, and the full run's tail may differ. Hmm. The test asserts exact step counts and matching control values between resumed tail and full tail. If subdivision inserts extra accepted increments in the full run but not in the resumed run (or vice versa), the histories won't align 1:1.\n\nHmm wait. But the test was written by me (the owner) in a prior run. The supervisor's adopted decision says \"Preserve every original scheduled control milestone, so half6+resume6 and full12 trace the same intended targets with any accepted subdivisions inserted. Do not claim exactly12 accepted steps.\"\n\nSo the test needs adjusting to compare milestone-subset histories: resumed steps must include the same milestone control values as the full tail (with subdivisions inserted anywhere). The comparison should match on the scheduled milestones: e.g., extract from both lists the steps whose control_value matches the scheduled milestone targets (within tolerance) and compare those; also require monotone plastic strain across all.\n\nBut wait — the user says \"Do not edit independent assessment scripts\" — the continuation test module is OWNED by me (\"Own tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3.py and a new tests/test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3_continuation.py\"). The independent assessment scripts are mesh_study.py, arc_reference.py, analytical_derivation.md. So I can edit the continuation tests.\n\nAlso the half test: `_assert_completed_dc_case(case, -0.10)` asserts control error etc. Fine.\n\nNow, will subdivision actually converge to -0.20? The physics question remains. The probe suggests severe ill-conditioning near step 11. If it's a true limit point (singular augmented matrix), subdivision will fail at min step and I must preserve evidence and report the obstruction. If it's just a step-size/nonlinearity issue, subdivision may work.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider the probe once more. Iteration 1: R≈0, constraint 0.0167. The Newton solve gave dlam=+1.56 and the new state has residual 4.2e5. Let me think about whether the augmented matrix could be near-singular: the dlam/duz trend was decreasing (2.4 at step 10). At a DC limit point (dLam/duz = 0), the augmented matrix [K -F; row 0] is singular. We're approaching it but not there yet (2.4 per unit... wait, dlam/duz = 0.04/0.0167 = 2.4 per meter? That's dlam per meter of uz. Hmm, that's small but not zero).\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the issue is the tangent K_red is garbage because of the geometric term sign. Let me re-derive more carefully whether reduce_tangent's geometric term has the right sign for the DC residual.\n\nDefine the map: u = φ(q) (apply_kinematics on T q + u0). J = du/dq.\n\nReduced equilibrium residual (production): R(q, lam) = J(u)^T [F_ext(u) - F_int(u)] where u = φ(q). (dead loads: F_ext independent of u.)\n\nNewton: dR/dq · dq + dR/dlam · dlam = -R.\n\ndR/dlam = J^T F_prop = F_prop_red. ✓.\n\ndR/dq = d/dq [J^T F_ext] - d/dq [J^T F_int]\n      = G(u, F_ext) - [J^T K_T J + G(u, F_int)]\nwhere G(u, v) := d/dq [J(φ(q))^T v] (v constant in q).\n\nProduction K_red = reduce_tangent(u, K_T, F_int - F_ext, None) = J^T K_T J + G(u, F_int - F_ext) = J^T K_T J + G(u, F_int) - G(u, F_ext) (if G linear in v).\n\nSo dR/dq = -K_red. ✓ (as before). Newton: -K_red dq + F_prop_red dlam = -R → K_red dq - F_prop_red dlam = R. ✓ production solves [K_red, -F_prop_red][dq,dlam] = [R, constraint]. Correct.\n\nIs G linear in v? G(u,v) = sum_b Theta_b^T H_b(theta_bar, e, v_b) Theta_b. H_b is the Hessian of the lever-arm map — linear in the force v_b. Yes.\n\nBUT — the skip condition: `if float(np.linalg.norm(f_b)) <= 1.0e-30: continue` — fine.\n\nHmm, so mathematically the tangent is right, ASSUMING geometric_tangent(u, v) really equals d/dq[J^T v]. Let me double check the sign of H_b vs the Jacobian. jacobian: delta_T[slave_u, :] = (A + r_skew) @ theta_proj, where A = rotating_mpc_lever_arm_jacobian(theta_bar, e). reduce_force: f_mod[slave_theta] += (A + r_skew)^T f_b.\n\nJ^T f = T^T f + delta_T^T f; delta_T^T f at column j = theta_proj[:,j]^T (A + r_skew)^T f_b. And reduce_force gives T^T f_mod = T^T f + (T rows at slave_theta)^T (A+r_skew)^T f_b. These match iff T's slave_theta rows == theta_proj. Presumably by construction. The R4 FD tests passed, which verified the complete reduced derivative including the geometric term at some state. So consistency is tested.\n\nOK so the assembly is right, and the failure is genuine numerical/physical: near-singular tangent. The observed behavior (dlam=1.56 vs expected ~0.03, residual explosion) is consistent with an ill-conditioned solve amplifying the small residual R=4.6e-7 along a near-null direction.\n\nHmm wait, actually — there's another possibility I should check: the probe's lstsq recovery of q. recovery_error 9.4e-17 — fine.\n\nAnd note: the probe's Newton at iteration 1 has R = 4.56e-7 which is BELOW criterion 7.9e-5, and constraint 0.0167 > criterion. So it takes a step. The step is garbage. If instead the solve were accurate, the step would be fine. So the question: is the augmented matrix ill-conditioned, or is the solve wrong? The probe used GENERAL factorization (PARDISO). If the matrix is ill-conditioned, PARDISO gives the exact-ish solution of the linear system — and the exact solution of a near-singular system IS large. So dq is genuinely large: the linear system itself says \"to move uz by 0.0167 while maintaining equilibrium, you need dlam=1.56 and a big dq\". That's the linearization's claim. If the tangent were accurate, that claim would be approximately true for small steps... but the true continuation shows dlam≈0.03. A 50× discrepancy means the tangent is NOT accurate at this state — i.e., the tangent is wrong somewhere, OR the matrix is near-singular so the solution is dominated by rounding noise in a near-null direction.\n\nDistinguish: if near-singular with singular value σ_min, the solution component along the near-null vector n is (n^T rhs)/σ_min. rhs = [R; 0.0167]. R~4.6e-7. If σ_min ~ 1e-6 (relative to norms ~1e5?), the R component alone gives dq_n ~ 0.46 along n... The constraint rhs also projects. It's plausible.\n\nPhysical interpretation of near-null: a plastic collapse mechanism where K_red q̇ ≈ F_prop·dlam... at collapse, K_red has a null mechanism direction m with F_prop·m... hmm.\n\nOK — enough theory. The adopted route is clear and I should implement it: GENERAL correction + bounded subdivision, then run and observe. The subdivision evidence will discriminate: if subdivided steps converge (residual after sub-step small, monotone), it was a step-size issue; if they still diverge at min step, it's a genuine obstruction to report.\n\nOne more design consideration for subdivision: on retry, restore q/lam AND committed_states candidates. The existing failure path does `_discard_nonlinear_state_candidate(committed_states)` then restores q/lam. For the rotating route, states_new = trial_states only set on convergence. The trial states from the failed attempt: `_assemble_nonlinear_system` returns trial_states; on failure iteration loop ends without committing. The existing failure path discards the candidate. For subdivision retry, do the same: discard candidate, restore q/lam, retry with smaller interval. Also `total_iterations` accounting continues (fine, record attempts).\n\nAlso cancellation safe points remain inside the iteration loop. Good.\n\nWhere do I put the \"attempts/cutbacks/stop reason\" record? info dict: `info[\"displacement_control_adaptation\"] = {\"attempts\": n, \"cutbacks\": n, \"min_step_fraction\": ..., \"stop_reason\": ...}`. And per-step history entries can include \"control_cutbacks\": cutbacks_this_step, \"control_attempts\": attempts_this_step. Adding keys to history dicts is additive; the restart contract may serialize history — check _static_restart_path_payload for strict history schema. Let me check how history is validated on restart (line 1147). If restart validation rejects unknown keys, adding keys could break the restart tests. Let me check.\n\nAlso the NonlinearStaticStep record: I keep one record per accepted increment (including inserted subdivisions), with step_index continuing sequentially. The step_index for inserted subdivisions: continue incrementing (step_index_offset + running count). But careful: `step_index` is currently `int(step_index_offset) + local_step_index`. With inserted subdivisions, I need a running accepted-step counter. The restart contract: `_static_step_from_restart` and step_index expectations — the resume test compares resumed steps to full tail by control_value/load_factor/plastic strain, not step_index... but the test I wrote asserts len equality which I'll relax to milestone matching. Also `history` entries have \"step_index\". For the resumed run, initial_history comes from the caller? No — the public restart route passes initial states, not history. The serialized checkpoint route is not used here.\n\nLet me check _static_restart_path_payload quickly to see if history keys are strictly validated (for the frozen Q4 checkpoint contract preservation).", "summary": []}
