{"id": "reasoning-1a656c06-53ac-5dd7-86e8-60d8abef677e-0", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792348561282, "updatedAt": 1792348561282, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Line 1027: `if len(history) != step_index: raise ValueError(\"static checkpoint force/displacement history is incomplete\")`. So the serialized checkpoint contract requires len(history) == step_index. With inserted subdivisions, if step_index counts accepted increments and history has one entry per accepted increment, this stays consistent as long as I keep them in lockstep. The frozen Q4 checkpoint validation is about the serialized route which we don't use here, but I must not break it. If step_index becomes a running accepted counter and history appends once per accepted increment, len(history)==step_index holds provided initial history/step_index offsets are consistent. Currently step_index = step_index_offset + local_step_index, and history starts as initial_history (len == step_index_offset presumably). With subdivisions, I'll use a running counter `accepted_step_count` so step_index = step_index_offset + accepted_step_count, and history appends per accepted increment. That preserves len(history) == step_index.\n\nWait, but does anything else depend on step_index == num_steps at completion? E.g., progress `completed=local_step_index, total=num_steps` — keep that as milestone-based (completed=local_step_index). The result's steps list length is now variable — tests assert len(snapshots)==len(steps) (fine) and the resume test len==6 (must relax to milestone matching).\n\nAlso `emit_progress` \"nonlinear_static_step\" events: the cancellation test cancels after 2 accepted events — with subdivisions, accepted sub-increments also emit events? If I emit progress per accepted increment (including subdivisions), cancellation after \"two accepted increments\" still works. The supervisor said \"Cancel after two accepted increments\" — fine either way.\n\nHmm, but wait: should inserted subdivisions emit step events and appear in steps/snapshots? \"with any accepted subdivisions inserted\" — yes, inserted into the step records. I'll emit progress per accepted increment too (keeps snapshots==steps==events consistent).\n\nNow, implementation. The current structure is deeply nested. Minimal restructuring plan:\n\nReplace the step loop body's target computation and add an inner subdivision while-loop. Concretely:\n\n```python\nadaptive_subdivision = rotating_mpc_map is not None and contact_manager is None\nmax_control_cutbacks = 8\nmin_subdivision_fraction = 1.0 / (2 ** max_control_cutbacks)  # of the scheduled increment\ncontrol_cutbacks = 0\ncontrol_attempts = 0\naccepted_step_count = 0\n```\n\nInside `for local_step_index in ...`:\n\n```python\nmilestone = initial_control + target_increment * local_step_index / num_steps\nsegment_start_control = <current control value>  # control value of current q\nscheduled_increment = target_increment / num_steps (could be zero? if target_increment==0... guard)\nsubdivisions = 1\ncutbacks_this_step = 0\nattempts_this_step = 0\nstep_accepted = False\nwhile True:\n    target = segment_start_control + (milestone - segment_start_control) / subdivisions\n    ... existing attempt code (augmentation loop, iteration loop) ...\n    if failure_reason is None:\n        # accepted: commit states, compute current, append step record/history/snapshot/progress\n        accepted_step_count += 1\n        if abs(current - milestone) <= tolerance * target_scale (or subdivisions == 1):\n            step_accepted = True\n            break\n        segment_start_control = current\n        # continue while-loop for remaining sub-intervals\n        continue... \n```\n\nHmm wait, there's a subtlety: `target = segment_start + (milestone - segment_start)/subdivisions` — after accepting the first sub-target, the remaining interval is [current, milestone], and I want to subdivide THE ORIGINAL interval into equal parts so milestones are hit exactly. Better: sub_targets = segment_start + (milestone - segment_start) * k / subdivisions for k=1..subdivisions. Track k. On acceptance, k += 1; if k == subdivisions: milestone reached. On failure: subdivisions *= 2, restart from segment_start with k=1? But then accepted sub-intervals... hmm, if we already accepted k=1 of 2 and then k=2 fails, we subdivide the REMAINING interval [target_1, milestone] — \"subdivide only that control interval\". So the failing interval is [current_control, milestone]. Subdivide it: new sub-targets within it.\n\nSimplest correct scheme:\n- Maintain `interval_start` (control value at the start of the current attempt = current accepted state) and `interval_end = milestone`.\n- Attempt target = interval_start + (interval_end - interval_start)/subdivisions_remaining... \n\nLet me simplify: attempt the FULL remaining interval [current, milestone] each time; on failure, halve the remaining interval: set `attempt_fraction *= 0.5`, target = current + (milestone - current) * attempt_fraction. On acceptance, if |current - milestone| > tiny: reset attempt_fraction = 1.0 and continue (the next attempt covers the new remaining interval; if it fails, halve again). This preserves milestones exactly (final target = milestone when attempt_fraction reaches... hmm, with repeated halving of the remaining interval, we approach the milestone geometrically and may never reach it exactly: current + (milestone-current)*f with f=1 after each acceptance — the last attempt always targets the full remaining interval, so if it succeeds we hit the milestone exactly. If it fails, halving. So milestones are hit exactly whenever an attempt at fraction 1.0 of the remaining interval succeeds. Termination: bound cutbacks per interval: sub-increment >= scheduled_increment * min_fraction... but the remaining interval shrinks; the bound should be on the absolute sub-increment: min_control_subincrement = |scheduled_increment| * 2**-8 (i.e., 1/256 of the scheduled per-step increment). If the required sub-increment < min, stop with failure_reason = \"minimum_control_step_reached\" (a recorded stop reason).\n\nWait, but the supervisor says \"retry within the existing min-step control and task budget\". The \"existing min-step control\" — the force route's min_step_fraction. There's no DC min-step currently. I'll define min sub-increment = scheduled_increment / 256 (8 halvings) as the bounded budget, and record it in info. That's a compatible internal choice.\n\nPer-interval or global cutback bound? \"bounded\" — I'll bound cutbacks per scheduled step (per milestone interval) at 8, and also track total. Actually per-interval halving with the absolute min-increment bound is cleaner: cutbacks allowed while (|milestone - current| * fraction) >= min_subincrement. Since remaining interval ≤ scheduled increment, at most ~8-ish halvings per interval; total bounded by 8 per milestone worst case. Fine.\n\nNow, the failure handling in the existing code: on failure it breaks out of the step loop with restore. I need to restructure so that on `maximum_iterations_reached` (and only that, only when adaptive enabled), we restore and retry with a halved interval; other failure reasons (singular_augmented_tangent, nonfinite, contact) break out as before. Also `maximum_augmentations_reached` — not applicable (no augmented contact manager in scope; augmentation_limit=1 when manager is None, so the for-else... wait: `for augmentation in range(1, augmentation_limit+1)` with augmentation_limit=1 runs once; the `else` clause of the for loop executes when no break — if the attempt succeeded, we `break` inside the iteration loop... hmm, actually the iteration loop breaks on convergence, then `if failure_reason is not None or augmented_contact_manager is None: break` — breaks out of the augmentation loop. So the for-else (maximum_augmentations_rea