pplied[:3] += lam * F_prop[dofs[:3]]\n        applied[3:] += lam * (cross(deformed, F_prop[dofs[:3]]) + F_prop[dofs[3:6]])\n    balance = support + applied\n```\n\nWait — but the transferred imbalance puts slave forces onto masters, including possibly root shell nodes (beam root slave transfers to shell root node). The root shell nodes are clamped. After transfer, all beam nodes have zero imbalance. The support wrench = sum over root nodes of their (transferred) imbalance = the reaction. Applied = tip loads. Equilibrium: support + applied = 0 (total balance; all other nodes have zero imbalance at equilibrium... no wait — non-root free nodes have imbalance ≈ 0 at convergence, so sum over root nodes of imbalance = -(applied) + O(residual). So balance = support + applied ≈ 0 with error ~ the free-node residuals. Tolerance: the test asserts balance_norm <= 1e-6 * applied_norm. Hmm — is that achievable? The support+applied sum equals -(sum of free-node imbalances) which is O(residual) ~ tolerance*reference. The residual criterion is 1e-7 relative. Free-node imbalance sum: the reduced residual is J^T(imbalance) ≤ 1e-7 relative, but the raw full imbalance vector could be larger in norm... Actually at convergence, the full imbalance f_int - lam F_prop is NOT zero at free nodes? It is zero at equilibrium for the physical system: f_int = f_ext at every free node. The solver converges the reduced residual J^T imbalance → 0; since J has full column rank over free dofs... the imbalance at free dofs maps through T (all constraint-eliminated). Hmm: imbalance in the full space satisfies T^T f_mod... the reduced residual = T^T (modified imbalance) ≈ 0. The free part: imbalance projected onto free DOFs ≈ 0 up to the convergence tolerance. The support+applied balance = sum over ALL nodes would be ~ total imbalance ~ small. With deformed arms and spatial moments, the wrench of the total imbalance about the origin: sum over all nodes [cross(x_i + u_i, f_i) + m_i]. This is the spatial wr