{"id": "reasoning-4e61e9c1-43ce-54d5-aebd-2d3546b69247-0", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792348857336, "updatedAt": 1792348857336, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Timed out at 150s with the direct case still grinding. The subdivision is likely converging slowly or grinding through cutbacks with 40-iteration attempts each. Each failed attempt costs up to 40 iterations (~40 assemblies+factorizations). 9 attempts × 40 = 360 iterations per milestone — very expensive.\n\nI need visibility: how far did it get? No output because pytest -q buffers... the \"F\" means the direct test FAILED (not hung) — wait, \"F\" printed means the direct test completed with failure quickly?, then interp was running when timeout hit. Let me check junit/stdout more. Actually stdout shows just \"F\" — direct failed. Why? Let me run the direct case alone with more diagnostics — or better, write a quick probe using the ep_dc_limit_probe-style script to see the subdivision behavior directly with per-step output. There's an existing diagnostics/ep_dc_limit_probe.py that runs full 12-step cases and prints JSON per case. Let me check what it does and run it 'direct' only, with per-step info including the new adaptation info.", "summary": []}
{"id": "effect-f5780fb7-e888-5b1d-a282-20522500b34d", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792348857357, "updatedAt": 1792348857452, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.bash", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.bash", "display": {"summary": "bash: cd \"C:/Github/ANYsolver\" && sed -n '1,80p' .development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/diagnostics/ep_dc_limit_probe.py", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Running", "message": "cd \"C:/Github/ANYsolver\" && sed -n '1,80p' .development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/diagnostics/ep_dc_limit_probe.py", "settledVerb": "Ran", "settledMessage": "cd \"C:/Github/ANYsolver\" && sed -n '1,80p' .development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/diagnostics/ep_dc_limit_probe.py", "statusText": "Running command"}, "kind": "shell", "input": {"command": "cd \"C:/Github/ANYsolver\" && sed -n '1,80p' .development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/diagnostics/ep_dc_limit_probe.py"}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"stdout": "\"\"\"Probe: why do the EP DC runs to -0.20 stop at limit?\n\nPrints, per coupling, the stop reason, achieved control value, and per-step\nhistory (control, iterations, residual, load factor) for the 12-step run to\n-0.20 and the 6-step run to -0.10, plus the resumed 6-step run.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport copy\nimport json\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"tests\"))\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3_continuation import (  # noqa: E402\n    _add_tip_load,\n    _build_ep_model,\n)\nfrom anysolver import DisplacementControl, solve_static_nonlinear  # noqa: E402\n\n\ndef run_case(coupling, target, num_steps, initial=None):\n    model, load, probe, grid = _build_ep_model(coupling)\n    _add_tip_load(model, load, grid)\n    kwargs = {}\n    if initial is not None:\n        kwargs = {\n            \"initial_displacements\": np.array(initial[0], dtype=float, copy=True),\n            \"initial_element_states\": copy.deepcopy(initial[1]),\n            \"equilibrate_initial_state\": False,\n        }\n    result = solve_static_nonlinear(\n        model, load, control=\"displacement\",\n        displacement_control=DisplacementControl(\n            node_id=probe, dof=\"uz\", target_displacement=target\n        ),\n        kinematics=\"corotational\", num_steps=num_steps,\n        max_iterations=40, tolerance=1.0e-8, **kwargs,\n    )\n    dofs = model.mesh.dof_manager.get_node_dofs(probe)\n    uz = float(np.asarray(result.displacements)[dofs[2]])\n    return {\n        \"coupling\": coupling, \"target\": target, \"num_steps\": num_steps,\n        \"restarted\": initial is not None,\n        \"status\": result.status,\n        \"stop_reason\": result.info.get(\"stop_reason\"),\n        \"failure_reason\": result.info.get(\"failure_reason\"),\n        \"status_category\": result.info.get(\"status_category\"),\n        \"converged\": result.converged,\n        \"load_factor\": result.load_factor,\n        \"uz_probe\": uz,\n        \"control_error\": abs(uz - target),\n        \"total_newton_iterations\": result.info.get(\"total_newton_iterations\"),\n        \"corotational_tangent\": result.info.get(\"corotational_tangent\"),\n        \"steps\": [\n            {\n                \"step\": s.step_index, \"lam\": s.load_factor,\n                \"iters\": s.iterations, \"res\": s.residual_norm,\n                \"ctrl\": s.control_value,\n                \"eps_p\": s.max_equivalent_plastic_strain,\n            }\n            for s in result.steps\n        ],\n    }\n\n\ndef main():\n    which = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else \"full\"\n    out = []\n    if which in (\"full\", \"all\"):\n        for coupling in (\"direct\", \"interp\"):\n            row = run_case(coupling, -0.20, 12)\n            print(json.dumps(row), flush=True)\n            out.append(row)\n    if which in (\"half\", \"all\"):\n        for coupling in (\"direct\", \"interp\"):\n", "stderr": "", "output": "", "truncated": false}, "outputText": "\"\"\"Probe: why do the EP DC runs to -0.20 stop at limit?\n\nPrints, per coupling, the stop reason, achieved control value, and per-step\nhistory (control, iterations, residual, load factor) for the 12-step run to\n-0.20 and the 6-step run to -0.10, plus the resumed 6-step run.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport copy\nimport json\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), \"..\", \"..\", \"..\", \"tests\"))\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom test_nonlinear_beam_shell_slice3_continuation import (  # noqa: E402\n    _add_tip_load,\n    _build_ep_model,\n)\nfrom anysolver import DisplacementControl, solve_static_nonlinear  # noqa: E402\n\n\ndef run_case(coupling, target, num_steps, initial=None):\n    model, load, probe, grid = _build_ep_model(coupling)\n    _add_tip_load(model, load, grid)\n    kwargs = {}\n    if initial is not None:\n        kwargs = {\n            \"initial_displacements\": np.array(initial[0], dtype=float, copy=True),\n            \"initial_element_states\": copy.deepcopy(initial[1]),\n            \"equilibrate_initial_state\": False,\n        }\n    result = solve_static_nonlinear(\n        model, load, control=\"displacement\",\n        displacement_control=DisplacementControl(\n            node_id=probe, dof=\"uz\", target_displacement=target\n        ),\n        kinematics=\"corotational\", num_steps=num_steps,\n        max_iterations=40, tolerance=1.0e-8, **kwargs,\n    )\n    dofs = model.mesh.dof_manager.get_node_dofs(probe)\n    uz = float(np.asarray(result.displacements)[dofs[2]])\n    return {\n        \"coupling\": coupling, \"target\": target, \"num_steps\": num_steps,\n        \"restarted\": initial is not None,\n        \"status\": result.status,\n        \"stop_reason\": result.info.get(\"stop_reason\"),\n        \"failure_reason\": result.info.get(\"failure_reason\"),\n        \"status_category\": result.info.get(\"status_category\"),\n        \"converged\": result.converged,\n        \"load_factor\": result.load_factor,\n        \"uz_probe\": uz,\n        \"control_error\": abs(uz - target),\n        \"total_newton_iterations\": result.info.get(\"total_newton_iterations\"),\n        \"corotational_tangent\": result.info.get(\"corotational_tangent\"),\n        \"steps\": [\n            {\n                \"step\": s.step_index, \"lam\": s.load_factor,\n                \"iters\": s.iterations, \"res\": s.residual_norm,\n                \"ctrl\": s.control_value,\n                \"eps_p\": s.max_equivalent_plastic_strain,\n            }\n            for s in result.steps\n        ],\n    }\n\n\ndef main():\n    which = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else \"full\"\n    out = []\n    if which in (\"full\", \"all\"):\n        for coupling in (\"direct\", \"interp\"):\n            row = run_case(coupling, -0.20, 12)\n            print(json.dumps(row), flush=True)\n            out.append(row)\n    if which in (\"half\", \"all\"):\n        for coupling in (\"direct\", \"interp\"):\n", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Ran", "message": "cd \"C:/Github/ANYsolver\" && sed -n '1,80p' .development/nonlinear-master-slave-coupling-20261017/diagnostics/ep_dc_limit_probe.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-1ec32330-1860-5626-8e24-13bc6be7a17c-0", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792348863794, "updatedAt": 1792348863794, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The limit probe prints per-case JSON including steps. Let me extend the probe output to include the adaptation info... but I shouldn't spend edits on the probe; it already prints steps (which now include inserted subdivisions) and stop_reason. The probe's run_case doesn't print the adaptation info, but steps show the inserted increments. Run it with \"direct\" only... the probe's `which` arg: \"full\" runs both couplings. I want just direct. Let me just run a quick inline script through the harness? The harness requires a command; I can run python -c... but \"Use shared harness for all solves\". OK — run the probe with a new evidence name; it will run direct first (prints per case with flush=True), and I can watch. But it runs both direct and interp for \"full\" — could exceed 120s. The prior ep-dc-limit-probe-1 run took 31.7s when both stalled early. Now with subdivision they may grind longer. Timeout 150 again? That just cost 150s. Budget: used 1124.8, remaining 2475.2, non-reserve 1575.\n\nHmm wait — before burning more budget, think about why the direct test FAILED (F) rather than hung. The direct test completed with a failure. That means the solve finished (stopped) and an assertion failed — e.g., still stopped_at_limit (subdivision exhausted → minimum_control_step_reached) OR completed but a postcheck failed. It failed within ~150s including interp grinding. Actually the F appeared quickly? stdout shows only \"F\" — pytest -q prints F as tests fail. The direct test failed at some point, then interp ran until timeout.\n\nI need the failure detail. The junit.xml may have been written? On timeout, the child is killed — junit not written. But stdout has \"F\" only; -q without -x... the failure detail is printed at the end (which never came).\n\nBetter: run ONLY the direct full test with a small timeout and capture the assertion. Actually the failure is likely `assert result.status == \"completed\"` → still stopping. The key question: where does it stop now, and what's the stop reason? The limit probe gives that directly. Run the probe \"direct\" — but the probe's main() with which=\"full\" runs both. Let me check the rest of main() — maybe there's a per-coupling arg. `which` in (\"full\",\"all\") → both couplings. No single-direct option. I could pass \"half\" (runs half cases for both couplings) — not what I want.\n\nI'll run the probe with \"full\" and timeout 120 — direct prints first (flush=True), so even if interp times out, I get direct's JSON in stdout. The probe prints each row as it completes. Direct previously stalled at step 11 after ~109 iters quickly (~15s). With subdivision, direct will grind: step 11 fails 40 iters, cutback... each attempt up to 40 iterations. If it eventually stops at min-step: 9 attempts × 40 = 360 iterations ≈ 3-4× the original 109-iteration cost... maybe 60-90s for direct alone. Then interp similar or worse. 120s timeout might capture direct only. That's the information I need. Go.", "summary": []}
