{"id": "reasoning-96145186-03b8-572d-9fd6-b49d9e94a371-0", "sessionId": "422bf0c3-019e-a3c2-0b19-09a4a66b19c5", "turnId": "turn-f62365e4f8dcef9678ebef32a78d524e", "createdAt": 1792349015189, "updatedAt": 1792349015189, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Very informative. The subdivision IS working mechanically: step 11 (milestone -0.1833) was reached via inserted increments: -0.175 (47 iters, 1 cutback), -0.1791666 (93 iters, more cutbacks), -0.18125 (139 iters), -0.1833333 (146 iters) — then the NEXT milestone (-0.20 target, milestone 12 = -0.20? wait, milestone 12 = -0.20; the last accepted increment is at -0.18333 = milestone 11. Then milestone 12 (-0.20) failed even at min sub-step → minimum_control_step_reached, stop at -0.18333.\n\nWait, look: the accepted increments after step 10: -0.175, -0.17917, -0.18125, -0.18333. These are subdivisions of the milestone-11 interval [-0.1667, -0.18333]: halves: -0.175 (1/2), then remaining [-0.175, -0.18333]: half = -0.17917, then half = -0.18125, then... -0.18333? Hmm, the last one at -0.18333 means the full remaining interval succeeded eventually. Interesting: so milestone 11 WAS reached via adaptive subdivision (4 accepted increments, 47+93+139+146 iterations — expensive but converging).\n\nThen milestone 12 (-0.20): failed at all subdivisions down to min → minimum_control_step_reached. The run stopped at -0.18333 with lam 2.668.\n\nNote the lam trajectory: 2.803 (step10) → 2.822 → 2.819 → 2.791 → 2.668. lam PEAKED around 2.822 near uz -0.175/-0.179 and is now DECREASING. That's a load-limit point crossed! In displacement control, crossing a snap-through limit point (dlam/duz = 0 → negative) is exactly where the augmented Newton [K,-F;row,0] becomes singular AT the limit point. Beyond the limit point, displacement control IS well-posed again (the augmented matrix is nonsingular for dlam/duz < 0)... but Newton from -0.1833 toward -0.20 must pass THROUGH the singular point (lam max ≈ 2.822 near uz ≈ -0.176?). Hmm wait — the peak: at -0.175 lam=2.8222, at -0.17917 lam=2.8191, at -0.18125 lam=2.7907, at -0.18333 lam=2.668. So the limit point (dlam/duz=0) is between -0.175 and -0.179. The accepted increments already crossed it! (lam decreasing while uz decreases further.) So the solve DID pass the limit point via subdivision — impressive. But then at milestone 12 (-0.20) it fails at ALL sub-steps down to 6.5e-5 m... \n\nHmm wait, actually — is that consistent? After crossing the limit point, the augmented matrix is nonsingular again (dlam/duz < 0). Newton should work again... unless there's ANOTHER limit point or the post-limit branch is being approached... The failure at milestone 12 even at tiny steps suggests the tangent is singular/near-singular at the CURRENT state (-0.18333) itself, or Newton diverges from every trial state.\n\nWait, but each attempt starts from the accepted state -0.18333 (equilibrated, res 5.5e-7). If the tangent at that state were fine, a small step (6.5e-5) should converge in a few iterations. Unless the tangent at -0.18333 is itself singular (K_red singular — a structural instability, e.g., plastic collapse mechanism where the tangent stiffness loses positive definiteness — the structure can deform at constant load). At a plastic collapse point, K_red has a zero eigenvalue; the augmented system [K,-F;row,0] is singular iff the collapse mechanism involves the control direction... Actually at collapse with displacement control: the system can undergo the mechanism at constant lam; the augmented matrix singular when the mechanism direction has no control-row component... \n\nThe lam decreasing while uz continues means we're on a softening branch — the structure is collapsing (plastic hinge forming at the root probably, eps_p 0.023 growing). The tangent K_red likely has a negative eigenvalue region... Newton with exact tangent handles negative stiffness fine (snap-through continuation works with displacement control past limit points as long as the augmented matrix stays nonsingular). The augmented matrix is singular exactly at dlam/duz = 0 points. Between -0.18125 and -0.18333, dlam/duz = (2.668-2.791)/0.00208 = -59 per m — strongly negative, not near zero. So at -0.18333 the augmented matrix should be well-conditioned... yet milestone 12 fails at all scales.\n\nHmm wait, maybe I misread: did milestone 12 attempts fail from -0.18333? The stop is minimum_control_step_reached with the last accepted at -0.18333 (milestone 11 reached). Milestone 12's interval is [-0.18333, -0.20], length 0.0167. min subincrement = 0.0167/256 = 6.5e-5. Attempts: 0.00833, 0.00417, ..., 6.5e-5 — 9 attempts, each up to 40 iterations, all failing = 360 iterations with zero progress. Total iterations 575: steps 1-10: 69; step 11 accepted increments: 47+93+139+146 = 425; remaining 575-69-425 = 81 iterations on milestone 12 attempts. Hmm, 81 < 360 — so attempts failed with FEWER than 40 iterations each: ~9 attempts × 9 iterations avg. That means the attempts are failing FAST — not max_iterations! Wait, but the retry condition only triggers on maximum_iterations_reached. If an attempt fails with, say, singular_augmented_tangent or nonfinite, no retry and immediate stop. But stop_reason is minimum_control_step_reached, which only comes from the max-iterations path. So attempts DID hit max_iterations... 81 iterations over 9 attempts = 9 each? But max_iterations=40 — an attempt ends at 40 if not converged. Unless... the attempt converges the residual but not the constraint? No...\n\nHmm wait, actually maybe not all 9 cutbacks happened. min subincrement check: fraction*0.5 >= min → cutback allowed. Interval 0.0167: fractions 0.5, 0.25, ..., 1/256 = 6.5e-5 ≥ 6.5e-5 ✓ (8 cutbacks), 1/512 < min ✗. So 9 attempts (1 full + 8 cutbacks), each ≤ 40 iters. 81 total → avg 9 iters per attempt. But an attempt only stops before 40 iters if failure_reason is set (singular/nonfinite/contact) — which would NOT retry... contradiction. Unless the attempts hit max_iterations=40 → 9×40=360 ≠ 81.\n\nLet me recount: total_newton_iterations=575. Steps 1-10: 6+7+7+7+7+7+7+7+7+7 = 69. Step 11 accepted: 47+93+139+146 = 425. 69+425 = 494. 575-494 = 81 for milestone 12. Hmm. But wait — \"iters\" per accepted step record = step_iterations accumulated across attempts of that milestone. Step 11's four records: 47 (first accepted increment: attempts 1 full failed 40 + 1 half succeeded 7 = 47 ✓), 93 (40+40+13? = 93: two failed full-ish attempts then success), 139, 146. So milestone 12: 81 iterations = e.g., 40+40+1? = 81: two failed attempts (80 iters) + third attempt 1 iter then...? No — if the third attempt failed at <40 iters with max_iterations it can't. Hmm, 40+40+1: the third attempt ran 1 iteration and then...? Actually maybe an attempt failed with singular_augmented_tangent at iteration 1 (factorize exception → break, no retry, stop). But stop_reason says minimum_control_step_reached...\n\nWait, maybe I'm wrong about the retry accounting: on retry, `continue` goes to the next while iteration; `attempts_this_step` increments... The iteration count: 40+40+1 = 81. The last attempt: 1 iteration then failure_reason = singular or nonfinite → no retry → but then failure_reason would be singular_augmented_tangent, not minimum_control_step_reached. Unless the last attempt hit the min-step bound: the sequence of cutbacks: attempt 1 (full, 40 iters, fail) → cutback; attempt 2 (half, 40, fail) → cutback; ... each failed attempt costs 40. 81 = 40+40+1 doesn't fit. 81 = 40+41? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively the attempts failed on max_iterations but with fewer iterations... max_iterations=40 is the cap; the for-else triggers only after all 40. So each max-iter attempt costs exactly 40. 81 isn't a multiple of 40. So something else: maybe some attempts failed with \"singular_augmented_tangent\" (factorize exception) — those don't retry and would end the run with that reason... but the recorded reason is minimum_control_step_reached.\n\nHold on — maybe my retry condition also catches the case where failure is max_iterations but the min-step bound is hit → sets minimum_control_step_reached. The attempts: 40×2 = 80, then the third attempt... 1 iteration. Hmm, what failure ends an attempt at 1 iteration? singular_augmented_tangent (factorize exception at iter 1) or nonfinite. Those set failure_reason but my retry only handles maximum_iterations_reached → falls to status assignment... wait no — look at my code: after the retry-if, there's the second if: `if failure_reason == \"maximum_iterations_reached\" and adaptive: failure_reason = \"minimum_control_step_reached\"`. If failure was singular_augmented_tangent, failure_reason stays singular → status = stopped_at_limit → break. stop_reason would be singular_augmented_tangent. But recorded is minimum_control_step_reached. Contradiction again.\n\nUnless... the 81 iterations include the LAST accepted increment's... no wait. Let me recount step 11's records: 47, 93, 139, 146. Hmm, sum 425. But actually maybe the \"iters\" of the last record (146) includes milestone-12 attempts? No — step_iterations resets per milestone (at for-level). Record 4 (ctrl -0.18333) is the last accepted increment of milestone 11; its 146 includes all milestone-11 attempts before it. Then milestone 12 attempts: 81.\n\nHmm, 81 = 40 + 40 + 1. What ends an attempt after 1 iteration? OH WAIT. I see — the convergence check! If an attempt at a tiny sub-target starts from an equilibrated state and the constraint is already satisfied within tolerance... no, constraint = target - current = 6.5e-5 > criterion 1e-8*target_scale (~1.8e-9)... hmm 6.5e-5 > 1.8e-9, not converged.\n\nAlternatively: singular factorize at iteration 1 of attempt 3 → failure_reason = singular_augmented_tangent → no retry → status stopped, reason singular. But recorded reason is minimum_control_step_reached...\n\nHmm, wait — maybe attempts 1 and 2 didn't run 40 each. Let me not over-infer; the exact breakdown doesn't change the conclusion: milestone 12 fails even at sub-steps down to the min bound, while milestone 11 succeeded via subdivision. The pattern (lam peaked ~2.822 around uz -0.176, now decreasing steeply: dlam/duz ≈ -59/m at the end, and the last accepted state has res 5.5e-7 — equilibrated) suggests we're past the first limit point but hitting another obstruction toward -0.20.\n\nActually — WAIT. Look at the lam values again: -0.18125 → lam 2.7907; -0.18333 → lam 2.668. dlam/duz = -59. Next milestone -0.20: if the trend continues, lam would drop to ~1.9 or lower... or there's another limit point (lam minimum) before -0.20 where dlam/duz = 0 again — a snap-BACK. At a snap-back point (local minimum of lam vs uz), the augmented DC matrix is ALSO singular (dlam/duz=0). Between the two, displacement control works. If there's a snap-back between -0.18333 and -0.20, pure displacement control CANNOT cross it — no step size helps; the constraint uz=-0.20 may be unreachable on this branch, or reachable only past the snap-back via arc-length.\n\nHmm, but attempts at 6.5e-5 sub-steps failed — if the tangent at -0.18333 were healthy (dlam/duz=-59, nonsingular), a 6.5e-5 step should converge in ~5-10 iterations. Failure at ALL scales from an equilibrated state with (presumably) a nonsingular tangent means the Newton diverges even for tiny steps — that's bizarre UNLESS the tangent is actually singular/near-singular at the current state.\n\nHmm wait, actually — is the tangent at -0.18333 near-singular? The last accepted increment took 146 iterations with heavy cutbacks — the state -0.18333 was reached only at fraction 1/8 or so of the remaining interval... The pattern of milestone 11: each successive sub-interval needed more cutbacks (47, 93, 139, 146 iters) — the difficulty is INCREASING as uz goes past -0.175. This looks like approaching a genuine singularity (snap-back or collapse) just beyond -0.18333. At the singularity, no subdivision helps.\n\nSo the physical picture: the EP direct case has a displacement-control limit point around uz ≈ -0.176 (lam max 2.822) which subdivision crossed, and now a second singularity (probably a snap-back in lam or full plastic collapse) shortly beyond -0.18333 blocks progress to -0.20. The interp case stopped even earlier (at -0.083 in the original run — wait, interp stopped at step 5-6 originally at -0.083; the interp case has lam 9.6 at -0.083 — much stiffer/stronger... its obstruction is elsewhere).\n\nHmm hold on, let me reconsider. Is this really physical, or is the tangent still wrong somewhere? The R4 FD tests verified the complete reduced derivative at SOME state — but maybe at larger rotations the work_conjugate tangent has an error... The supervisor's adopted position: \"If bounded subdivision fails, preserve that evidence and report the specific obstruction, not physical collapse.\" We now have discriminating evidence: subdivision crossed one limit point (lam max at uz≈-0.176) but is blocked beyond uz≈-0.1833 at all sub-step sizes down to 6.5e-5 m.\n\nBefore concluding, one more diagnostic is warranted (decision-oriented): does the Newton at a tiny sub-step from the last accepted state diverge with a healthy or singular tangent? The step11 probe could be adapted... but budget: used 1245, remaining 2355, non-reserve 1455. Turns: I'm around turn ~20 of 60. The user's narrow outcome: \"finish DC failure repair and EP acceptance tests, fix physical root wrench test, retain all evidence... Then GENERAL augmented matrix correction and bounded rotating contactfree DC subdivision if needed.\"\n\nThe subdivision is implemented and discriminating evidence exists. The question: is the -0.20 target physically reachable in displacement control on this model? If there's a snap-back, no internal repair can reach it without arc-length — that's a physics/contract question for Astra, not something I may change (no physics changes allowed).\n\nBut wait — before reporting an obstruction, double-check one alternative explanation: maybe the Newton failures are due to the tangent being wrong at large plastic strain (work_conjugate tangent inconsistency at finite rotation+plasticity). A discriminating check: at the last accepted state (-0.18333), compute the reduced residual and tangent, and check the tangent against a finite-difference of the residual (like the R4 test but at this state). If the tangent is consistent (FD matches), the obstruction is physical (singular tangent/snap-back). If not, there's a defect to repair.\n\nThat's a focused, decision-oriented probe. The step11 probe already has the machinery; I can write a small variant: run the prefix to -0.18333 (using the production solver with subdivision — 575 iterations, ~60-90s), then at the final state do a central-FD check of dR/dq along a few directions vs the assembled K_red. Hmm, cost: prefix ~90s + FD ~10 assemblies ≈ 30s. ~2 min. Worth it — it discriminates \"tangent defect\" vs \"physical singularity\" and is exactly the kind of evidence Astra needs.\n\nActually — cheaper and even more discriminating: at the final state, examine the augmented matrix's conditioning: compute the smallest singular value of K_red and of the augmented matrix, and the dlam/duz predicted by the linear solve. If the augmented matrix at the accepted state is near-singular (σ_min tiny), physical snap-back approach. Plus an FD tangent check along the control direction. Let me write this probe.\n\nActually, let me think about what the most useful evidence is for Astra's decision:\n1. Is the tangent at the blocked state consistent with the residual (FD check)? → production defect vs physics.\n2. Is the augmented matrix near-singular at the blocked state? → snap-back/limit obstruction.\n3. What does the lam(uz) curve look like — is -0.20 reachable at all on any branch (e.g., does lam continue decreasing, suggesting the target is on the post-snap-back branch)?\n\nThe probe: prefix solve to -0.20 with subdivision (stops at