efine R(q, lam) = mpc_map.reduce_force(u(q), lam*F_prop - F_int(u(q)))  [same as production]\n- K_red(q, lam) = mpc_map.reduce_tangent(u, K_T, F_int - lam*F_prop, None)\n- FD: pick directions e_i (a few reduced coordinates + the control-relevant direction): central diff (R(q+h e)-R(q-h e))/(2h) vs K_red[:, i]. Report relative errors. h ~ 1e-6 * scale.\n- SVD of K_red dense: smallest 3 singular values.\n- Augmented matrix [K_red, -F_prop_red; row_jac, 0]: smallest singular values.\n- Tiny Newton step: constraint = 6.5e-5 (min sub-step), solve, report ||dq||, dlam, then the actual residual at the trial state (one step, no iteration) — shows how bad the linear prediction is.\n\nAlso print dlam/duz estimate from the solve.\n\nCost: prefix ~90s (direct) + ~20 assemblies (each assembly ~0.1-0.3s? 575 iterations took ~100s → ~0.17s per assembly+solve) + SVDs trivial. Fine within 120s? Direct prefix took most of 120s in the probe run... risky. Give timeout 180 for this probe (it's a diagnostic, per-case 120s guidance is for tests; the harness timeout is my choice — previous probes used 120; the combined CR batch timed out at 120 and was preserved. I'll use 180 and accept the charge).\n\nHmm wait, actually — the direct full run in ep-dc-limit-probe-2 didn't finish within 120s?? It printed direct's JSON... so direct completed within 120s (575 iterations). Then interp was still running at timeout. So direct ≈ 60-100s. The block probe adds ~20 assemblies ≈ +10s. 180s timeout is safe for direct. Interp: unknown — its obstruction is earlier (-0.083); with subdivision it may grind longer per milestone... The original interp stalled at step 6 (77 iters, fast). With subdivision, interp will also cross/cutback... unknown duration. Run interp separately with 180s too.\n\nActually — wait. Let me reconsider the priority. The EP acceptance requires reaching -0.20 for BOTH couplings. If the obstruction is physical (snap-back), the acceptance target itself is unreachable in pure displacement control and that's a contract decision for Astra. My job: produce the discriminating evidence. The tangent-FD check is the critical piece (defect vs physics). Let me write the probe to do the FD check at the terminal state for whichever coupling, plus the singularity measures.\n\nOne more thought — maybe I should ALSO check the tangent consistency at a HEALTHY state (e.g., -0.10) to validate the probe methodology (FD matches there, fails at -0.183 if defective). The probe can do the FD check at both the half state and the terminal state. The half solve to -0.10 is cheap (6 steps, no cutbacks). Good — that gives a within-run control.\n\nLet me write the probe now. Keep it compact.\n\nAlso note for later: the direct case DID reach milestone 11 (-0.18333) — so the half test (-0.10) and resume (to -0.20) — resume will stop at -0.18333 too. The EP acceptance gates can't pass until/unless the obstruction is resolved. The wrench fix and other tests (preyield, half to -0.10, rejected restart, cancelled resume, stopped snapshots) can still pass. The full/resume tests will fail with the obstruction documented.\n\nLet me now write the block probe.", "summary": []}
