augmented matrix is EXTREMELY ill-conditioned at the terminal state. The second-smallest is 1.4e4. So there's ONE near-null direction of the augmented matrix with σ_min = 0.0106.\n\nThe near-null direction of [K_red, -F; row, 0]: a vector (dq, dlam) with K_red dq ≈ F_prop dlam and row_jac·dq ≈ 0. That's a displacement-direction mechanism at constant... this is the classic displacement-control limit-point indicator: the augmented matrix is singular exactly when dlam/duz = 0 (limit point in lam) — the null vector has dlam ≠ 0? Hmm, let me think: at a lam-limit point (dlam/duz = 0), the solution branch has dq direction with row·dq ≠ 0 and dlam = 0... The augmented matrix singular ⟺ exists (dq,dlam) ≠ 0 with K_red dq = F_prop dlam and row·dq = 0. \n\nThe tiny-step solve: dq_norm 2.66e-4 for a 6.5e-5 constraint — 4× amplification, dlam = -0.0061 (dlam/duz = -94). The trial residual after the tiny step: 6.35 — vs criterion ~1e-4·8e3 = 0.8. Hmm, trial residual 6.35 — that's ~8× above the convergence criterion but NOT catastrophic. Interesting — so a tiny step from the terminal state gives residual 6.35, which Newton should then reduce... but the attempts failed at 40 iterations. Hmm, wait — the trial residual 6.35 after one linear step from an equilibrated state. The linear prediction says the residual should be ~0 (R was ~5.5e-7·... wait, the base residual at the terminal state: R ≈ 5.5e-7 relative → absolute ~5.5e-7 × 8e3 ≈ 4.4e-3?). The actual residual after the step is 6.35 — dominated by second-order terms: ½ dq^T H dq with ||dq||=2.66e-4... that implies enormous curvature H ~ 6.35/(0.5·(2.66e-4)²) ~ 1.8e8 — comparable to K_red's largest singular value 8.5e9? Within two orders. Plausible for a thin shell with plasticity.\n\nBut the KEY number: σ_min(aug) = 0.0106 vs σ_max 8.5e9 — condition number 1e12. The linear solve amplifies rounding errors by up to 1e12·eps... wait no — the solve is fine mathematically; the issue is that the near-null direction makes the Newton s