is EP case: shell 2x1, loads uz, eccentricity z... The deformations include rotations rx (bending about x?) — the tip load uz on a cantilever-ish panel bends about y? The shell spans x∈[0,1], y∈[-0.12,0.12]; uz loads bend about y-axis (ry). Planar-ish. The map's documented scope: \"planar finite rotations and tested states\". The fold occurs at uz ~-0.18 (huge, 18% of span) with eps_p 0.023 — extreme. Whether the fold is a true physical snap-back of the elastic-plastic shell-stiffener model or a manifold artifact — I can't decide; that's Astra's scientific call. My evidence characterizes it precisely.\n\nHmm, actually — one more discriminating check I could do cheaply: does the fold exist in the ELASTIC (preyield-scale) model? If the elastic model (scale 1e4) reaches -0.20 smoothly, the fold is plasticity-driven (physical collapse-like). If the elastic model ALSO folds at -0.18, it's a geometric nonlinearity (snap-back) — still physical. Either way it's the model's manifold. Not needed for the obstruction report; skip (budget).\n\nWait, actually there IS one more relevant check: the arc_reference/mesh_study are Astra's oracles — out of my hands. OK.\n\nNow — the immediate work:\n1. Verify the wrench fix + the passing subset of the continuation module. Run the module excluding the -0.20-completion tests? The module run takes ~100s+ but now the full/resume tests will grind through subdivision attempts (expensive! direct full = 575 iters ~60-90s; interp ~50s; resume runs similar; cancelled reuse similar). A full module run could take 5-8 minutes and mostly produce known failures. EXPENSIVE. Budget: used 1446.8, remaining 2153.2, non-reserve 1253.2s. Turns: ~26 of 60.\n\nDecision-oriented: I need (a) evidence that the wrench fix works (half case completed + balance ~0), (b) the module's pass/fail state for the handoff. For (b), a full module run is the honest integrated check, but costly. Alternative: run the achievable subset now (preyield, half, rejected, stopped) + verify wrench via the half cases; and run the full module ONCE at the end (final verification phase) accepting ~6-8 min... that might exceed per-case 120s harness timeout! The harness timeout applies to the whole child. A full module run with subdivision grinding could take 400-600s. I'd need --timeout 600+. The brief says per-case 120s — the previous full-module run was 100s. With the repair, failing cases grind longer (direct full 90s, interp 113s, resume direct ~90s, resume interp ~110s, cancelled ~90s, plus passing ~60s) → total ~500-550s. One child, timeout 600. That's a big chunk of the remaining 1253s non-reserve. Hmm.\n\nAlternative plan: mark the -0.20-dependent tests as expected to fail at the documented obstruction? NO — cannot weaken gates.\n\nLet me think about what maximizes value within budget:\n1. Wrench verification: run just the half tests (2 cases, ~30-40s) — verifies wrench fix + half completion + plasticity.\n2. Run the remaining achievable tests (preyield 4, rejected 1, stopped 1) — ~40s.\n3. Full module integrated run with timeout 600 — the definitive count for handoff. ~550s.\n\nTotal ~630s + slack. Non-reserve 1253s. Feasible but tight with the 900 reserve untouched. Also I should rerun the affected repairs-module tests (R5 DC tests) — the repairs module (16 tests, 40s) — my DC changes affect R5 (and R4? R4 is force-control derivative — unaffected; R5 uses DC). Rerun the full repairs module (40s) — justified (source changed).\n\nAlso the preyield4 controls in the continuation module are force-control — unaffected by DC changes but they're in the module run anyway.\n\nPlan order:\n1. Repairs module rerun (16 tests, ~40s) — confirms no regression from the DC restructure on the passing 16.\n2. Continuation half+preyield+rejected+stopped subset (~60s) — wrench verification.\n3. Full continuation module (timeout 600) — integrated evidence.\n4. Update tests? No. Checkpoint + handoff.\n\nActually wait — for step 2/3 overlap: the full module includes